const mindCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The mind–body problem', note: 'Mental states seem different in kind from physical states (a pain is private and qualitative; a brain state is measurable and public). How do they relate? Descartes said two substances (dualism); modern science says one.' },
    { math: '2 · Physicalism and its challenge', note: 'Mental states ARE brain states (identity theory) — but "what it is like" (Nagel\'s bat, 1974) resists capture in third-person science. Mary the colour scientist (Jackson): knowing everything physical about red doesn\'t tell you what red looks like — or does it (the "ability hypothesis" reply)?' },
    { math: '3 · Functionalism: mind as software', note: 'Mental states are defined by their FUNCTIONAL role (inputs → outputs → other states), not their material. Implausible consequence embraced: a sufficiently good simulation could think — the philosophical licence behind AI.' },
    { math: '4 · Consciousness is the stress test', note: 'Every theory above must explain subjective experience ("the hard problem" — Chalmers). Global workspace, higher-order theories and integrated information (the neuroscience ladder) are the physicalist answers; they explain the functions, and argue about the rest.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/intermediate/philosophy-of-mind">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Philosophy of mind asks <strong>what mental states are and how they relate to brains</strong> —
    the question underneath AI, psychiatry, and every claim that "we are just neurons". The
    modern mainstream is physicalist, but the subjective character of experience keeps the
    debate genuinely open — and keeps AI's philosophical licence under review.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="dualism, physicalism, functionalism, and the hard problem stress test" widget="equationStep" config={mindCfg} />

  <h2>The Chinese Room and its many replies</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Searle (1980):</strong> a man follows rules to answer Chinese messages without understanding Chinese — symbol manipulation isn't understanding. therefore computers (as syntax engines) can't genuinely think.</li>
    <li><strong>The systems reply:</strong> the MAN doesn't understand, but the whole SYSTEM does — Searle's man is a part, not the mind. The debate mirrors every current LLM discussion: is scale-plus-training understanding, or just very good symbol manipulation?</li>
    <li><strong>Functionalism's testable edge:</strong> if mental states are functional roles, the question "could an LLM have experiences?" becomes empirical-ish: does it realise the right functional organisation? The measurement problem is genuinely hard (the consciousness lesson continues).</li>
    <li><strong>Why it matters now:</strong> AI moral status, neural-privacy rights, and psychiatry's mind–body integration all inherit this debate's vocabulary.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Nagel, "What Is It Like to Be a Bat?" (1974); Searle, BBS (1980); Chalmers, The Conscious Mind (1996); Clark & Chalmers, "The Extended Mind" (1998).</p>
</TopicPage>
